WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.049 --> 00:00:01.049
Good morning, John.

00:00:01.049 --> 00:00:05.040
I'm glad that you're enjoying the majestic
and exotic Amsterdam so much. But here in

00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:09.420
Missoula, Montana we have something that I
don't think that you have in your little apartment

00:00:09.420 --> 00:00:14.510
in Amsterdam, and that is video games. I know
that they...have... but I want to talk about

00:00:14.510 --> 00:00:18.029
video games today! I've been playing Portal
2 on my gaming channel, and it's super fun.

00:00:18.029 --> 00:00:21.130
I've also been playing Lego Harry Potter and
Assassin's Creed. I'm about to finish up Assassin's

00:00:21.130 --> 00:00:24.140
Creed, so I'm trying to figure out what I'm
going to do next. I grew up in a different

00:00:24.140 --> 00:00:28.819
video game age, and while you, uh, sort of
had control over the Nintendo when it wasn't

00:00:28.819 --> 00:00:34.149
filled with poop, I had to play a different
kind of video game because you were bogarting

00:00:34.149 --> 00:00:35.149
the grey box.

00:00:35.149 --> 00:00:39.559
So I was playing a different sort of game,
a two dimensional game, a DOS sort of game.

00:00:39.559 --> 00:00:45.809
Simulation games... that actually simulated
things. And so with the hope that I will educate

00:00:45.809 --> 00:00:49.739
people who were not around during this era
and provide a certain amount of nostalgia

00:00:49.739 --> 00:00:54.629
for those of us who are old enough to remember
it, I am going to talk today about the best

00:00:54.629 --> 00:00:56.660
simulation games of the 1990s.

00:00:56.660 --> 00:01:00.739
So Maxis was a company that pretty much created
the simulation game. They started with SimCity,

00:01:00.739 --> 00:01:04.790
and then they tried to do everything, and
they ended up doing the Sims which is, I think,

00:01:04.790 --> 00:01:08.970
the most popular simulation game of all time
where you get to pretend to be a person! Which

00:01:08.970 --> 00:01:12.910
is weird; you would think people would want
to pretend to be something else besides a

00:01:12.910 --> 00:01:14.800
person since they get to be a person every
day, but yeah.

00:01:14.800 --> 00:01:20.340
So Maxis made SimLife where you got to sort
of create evolutionary biology. And they created

00:01:20.340 --> 00:01:24.810
SimAnt where you simulated an ant colony and
got to take over somebody's house. That was

00:01:24.810 --> 00:01:30.090
weird. And SimHealth which was a simulation
of America's health care system which, unsurprisingly,

00:01:30.090 --> 00:01:34.930
was a nightmare and not at all fun. It wasn't
like "SimSurgery", it was like "SimHMOs."

00:01:34.930 --> 00:01:39.080
And then there was one of my favorites which
was SimFarm which is like FarmVille except

00:01:39.080 --> 00:01:42.920
actually a simulation of a farm. Like, for
example, if you don't feed your pigs, they

00:01:42.920 --> 00:01:46.740
die. You have to borrow money from the bank,
and you can sell your crops on the futures

00:01:46.740 --> 00:01:51.290
market. And the only way to get rich is to
plant a cash crop monoculture because you're

00:01:51.290 --> 00:01:52.930
a farmer, and that's how it is!

00:01:52.930 --> 00:01:57.780
And then there's Sid Meier's Civilization,
a game that has now had five incarnations,

00:01:57.780 --> 00:02:02.990
all of them REALLY good. And there's different
ways to "win" the game. You can kill everyone

00:02:02.990 --> 00:02:07.560
which is, you know, enjoyable for some people.
Or you can find peace and a strength in a

00:02:07.560 --> 00:02:12.930
diverse planet with lots of different people.
Maybe the warlike people will be eradicated.

00:02:12.930 --> 00:02:15.950
People who love peace can live together in
harmony!

00:02:15.950 --> 00:02:20.640
SimEarth, another Maxis game, was a simulation
of the entire planet, like CO2 levels and

00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:23.750
ocean currents. The actual interaction that
you had with the game was pretty limited,

00:02:23.750 --> 00:02:28.490
but really, it was just a textbook in the
form of a game which was good for me because

00:02:28.490 --> 00:02:29.560
I was a little nerd!

00:02:29.560 --> 00:02:34.090
And now I'm going to end with SimCity because,
of course, SimCity and SimCity 2000: best

00:02:34.090 --> 00:02:37.530
simulation games of all time! I got SimCity
when I was nine years old, so at that ripe

00:02:37.530 --> 00:02:41.870
young age, I got to become the mayor of Heresville,
decide whether I wanted to be a destructive

00:02:41.870 --> 00:02:46.710
or benevolent leader. I was always benevolent.
I, I wish I could say I was destructive. I

00:02:46.710 --> 00:02:50.740
never... I never do the evil thing in video
games. People who have watched me play Assassin's

00:02:50.740 --> 00:02:53.950
Creed are laughing right now. That's because
I get bored with Assassin's Creed and I just

00:02:53.950 --> 00:02:54.950
start stabbing people.

00:02:54.950 --> 00:02:59.230
I can hardly conceive of the amount of stuff
that I just implicitly know about the world

00:02:59.230 --> 00:03:02.540
because of simuliza--SIMULIZATION! That's
the second time I've done that! That's the

00:03:02.540 --> 00:03:05.990
second take in which I said "simulization!"
But more than the truth about the world and

00:03:05.990 --> 00:03:09.740
the facts that I learned from "simulization"
games-- what I really love about them is that

00:03:09.740 --> 00:03:12.990
often there's no winning; there's no goal.
You can't beat SimCity.

00:03:12.990 --> 00:03:17.450
Because that's one of the weird things about
life; like, we don't know why--what we're

00:03:17.450 --> 00:03:22.240
doing here. We don't know what we're supposed
to do. Different parts of society and different

00:03:22.240 --> 00:03:26.600
cultures put different weights on different
things, whether it's having a healthy family

00:03:26.600 --> 00:03:31.840
or fancy cars or comparing yourself to your
coworkers or making lots of money. But unless

00:03:31.840 --> 00:03:35.700
you're Charlie Sheen, there's no winning.
You never get to beat life. Knowing I get

00:03:35.700 --> 00:03:40.130
to decide for myself what success is, that's
one of the most important lessons I ever learned.

00:03:40.130 --> 00:03:42.520
And I learned it from playing simulation games
so... fancy that!

00:03:42.520 --> 00:03:46.000
John, I know that you were busy playing Nintendo
games while all of this was going on, so I

00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:51.210
hope this wasn't too boring for you. Uh, but
yes! That is how I became a nerd!

00:03:51.210 --> 00:03:51.990
I'll see you on Friday.

